#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8041938c-b394-5c87-9c43-eb0775333a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321141} {:answer/id #uuid "b2975091-274c-575a-9ca4-99f242ca3d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321142} {:answer/id #uuid "e23dcaae-343e-5c6f-ab58-e9e96baff3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2818cb-bca7-5dbf-ab48-fe00791e051c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321144} {:answer/id #uuid "28774533-52cb-5376-a3bd-b7e727f3538f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e23dcaae-343e-5c6f-ab58-e9e96baff3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321143} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca9088a-3574-5fb0-a2c2-bca04f47d657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4bd1c51-7cdb-5faf-a2e7-d35b9e8b6eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143077" :question/problem "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a food container decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the container $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a food container **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the temperature.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature $T$* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the temperature $T.$* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$"}]}
